  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $2$ units, so the distance equals $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/6759fd05bd968e4e3eb994ea05214eb344b57b3bc4c74502a4a3fbbca5602127.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/bebc13148ee02764514299e0c4147f4f3637fbf7a0ef6879e9b2e870364283c5.png)\n\nThe point $(-4,6)$ is at a distance of $6$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-4,4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $6-4=2.$",
